    "result_text": "The full build is green. Let me summarize what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the soft-match \"possible duplicate\" rule for owner creation. When a new owner is not a hard identity duplicate but shares an existing owner's `lastName` and `postcode` with a *different* telephone, it is still created and flagged with `possibleDuplicate: true` / `possibleDuplicateOf: <matching owner id>`; otherwise `possibleDuplicate: false`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added persisted fields `possibleDuplicate` (Boolean, `possible_duplicate`) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer, `possible_duplicate_of`) with getters/setters, following the existing derived-field pattern.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `possibleDuplicate` and `possibleDuplicateOf` as read-only properties on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries them and they're returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n3. **Schema DDL** (all four dialects: `hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the two columns to the `owners` table. Tests run on `hsqldb`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added `findPossibleDuplicateOf(lastName, postcode, normalizedTelephone)`: after the hard-duplicate check, it searches existing owners for one sharing the normalized last name and the postcode but with a different telephone, returning the lowest matching id (deterministic) or `null`. The result sets `possibleDuplicate` (true iff a match) and `possibleDuplicateOf` before saving.\n\n5. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 the entity\u2192DTO direction auto-maps the two fields by name; added `@Mapping(..., ignore = true)` for both on `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`, matching how the other server-derived fields are handled.\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest.coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate` passes, and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS` (Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).\n\nOne design note worth flagging: I keyed the soft match on requiring a *different* telephone, taken directly from the spec wording (\"with a different telephone\"). Since the hard-duplicate check runs first, an exact-identity collision is already rejected before this logic runs.",
